well the government's big pro-eu leaflet  is out with lots of facts and figures  about the benefits of membership of the  European Union but do they all stack up  according to the Sky News specialist  first of all let's take economics and  there's a big claim about forty four  percent of UK exports going across into  the EU now there is true but it's worth  noting it's in decline a few years ago  the number was about fifty percent  secondly there's the claim amount being  inside the common market and how that  makes life cheaper for businesses now of  course that's true as well but even if  the UK were outside the EU and have an  arrangement a bit like Norway we'd still  be inside the common market finally  there's a big fact 540 billion pounds  worth of foreign direct investment going  into the UK over the course of the past  decade the big question though is  whether that investment would have  happened anyway even if we were outside  Europe economic issues are relevant many  of the key social questions too by far  the most incendiary of which is  immigration the leaflet says that if we  left the EU much like Norway which isn't  a member but still needs access to that  crucial EU single market we would still  have to accept EU immigration others say  we could cut it down by a hundred  thousand a year if we left the EU the  truth is we don't know but it would  depend on exactly what we could  negotiate in order to secure access to  that crucial market and remember  immigrations the two-way street the  leaflet warns that travel perks we  currently enjoy the ability to travel  and work widely in Europe at a cheap  cost would become harder and more  expensive in the event of a brexit and  there's no question that in the short  term at least that claim is true and  what about things that aren't mentioned  in the leaflet at all employment rights  like working hours and parental leave  protection from discrimination and  equality those are things that are  currently guaranteed by Brussels and if  we left the EU we'd be at the mercy of  whichever British government was in  place it's very hard to measure security  when we're trying to assess whether or  not Britain would be safer inside the EU  or outside the EU one thing that will  never ever change and that is that  Britain is an island which does make  borders easy to protect much easier than  on  continental Europe by being a member of  the EU Britain is also a part of Europol  which gives police forces here access to  data and fingerprint records from other  police forces around Europe and that  would cease to be the case if Britain  were to leave the European Union the  leaflet also correctly points out that  around a thousand criminals have been  arrested on the continent and brought  back to the UK to face justice using  what's known as the European Arrest  Warrant however only one terrorist has  been detained using those powers  ultimately no one yet has made the case  decisively that proves that Britain  would be safer if it were to leave the  EU and as ed and a foie have pointed out  this leaflet for its facts all its  figures doesn't decide the case one way  or the other
 

